Android 15 / API 35: do not forward a guest instance name to a non-isolated proxy service.

SYMPTOM
    A cloned Chrome renders its first-run UI, then aborts roughly 44 s after launch,
    before any renderer exists. On the CPH2605 (Android 15), from the browser process:

        E ActivityManagerStub: Caused by: java.lang.IllegalArgumentException:
            Can't use instance name '0' with non-isolated non-sdk sandbox service
            'top.niunaijun.blackbox.proxy.ProxyService$P1'
          at android.app.IActivityManager$Stub$Proxy.bindServiceInstance(IActivityManager.java:6490)
          at com.android.server.am.ActivityManagerService.bindServiceInstance(ActivityManagerService.java:15388)

        E chromium: [ERROR:jni_android.cc:142] Crashing due to uncaught Java exception
        E cr_JniAndroid: Handling uncaught Java exception
          at org.chromium.content.browser.ChildProcessLauncherHelperImpl.createAndStart
        F chromium: [FATAL:jni_android.cc:203] Uncaught Java exception in native code ...
        F libc: Fatal signal 5 (SIGTRAP) ... tid (Chrome_ProcessL)

    Chrome does not catch this. The browser process dies with SIGTRAP, so the guest
    never reaches a New Tab page. Measured across four attempts: 42-47 s lifetime,
    zero renderer processes, zero GPU processes.

WHAT CHROME ASKS FOR
    Chrome declares 40 sandboxed child services in base.apk, each isolated:

        <service android:name="org.chromium.content.app.SandboxedProcessService0"
                 android:process=":sandboxed_process0"
                 android:isolatedProcess="true"
                 android:useAppZygote="true"
                 android:exported="false"
                 android:permission="com.android.chrome.permission.CHILD_SERVICE" />

    It starts each one with Context.bindIsolatedService(..., instanceName), passing the
    slot number ("0", "1", ...). Against Chrome's own isolated service that is legal:
    the platform names such a process <processName>:<instanceName> and gives it a uid in
    the isolated 99000+ range. Observed on this device from host WebView:

        com.google.android.webview:sandboxed_process0:org.chromium.content.app.SandboxedProcessService0:0
        at uid 99017

WHAT BCORE SUBSTITUTES
    Bcore resolves the guest service and rewrites the bind to one of its own proxy
    services. Those are declared in Bcore's manifest as ordinary services:

        <service android:name=".proxy.ProxyService$P1"
                 android:exported="true"
                 android:process=":p1" />

    There is no android:isolatedProcess anywhere in that manifest. So the component that
    actually reaches ActivityManagerService is not isolated, while the instance name from
    the guest is still attached to the call. ActivityManagerService rejects exactly that
    combination, and it is right to: an instance name only has meaning for an isolated or
    SDK-sandbox service.

ANDROID 15 / API 35 BINDER METHOD NAME
    Context.bindIsolatedService(...)                                <- public SDK
        invokes
    IActivityManager.bindServiceInstance(...)                       <- @hide Binder method

    There is no IActivityManager.bindIsolatedService on API 35. Read from the device's own
    /system/framework/framework.jar, bindIsolatedService is declared only on the caller
    side -- android.content.Context, android.app.ContextImpl, android.content.ContextWrapper
    and android.app.ReceiverRestrictedContext -- and not on android.app.IActivityManager.
    The observed crash stack agrees:

        at android.app.ContextImpl.bindIsolatedService(ContextImpl.java:2095)
        at android.app.ContextImpl.bindServiceCommon(ContextImpl.java:2204)
        at $Proxy27.bindServiceInstance(Unknown Source)

OLD BCORE REGISTRATION
    Two sibling hooks. Only one of them strips the instance name:

        @ProxyMethod("bindServiceInstance")
        public static class bindServiceInstance extends MethodHook {
            protected Object hook(...) { return BindServiceCommon(who,method,args,7); }
        }

        @ProxyMethod("bindIsolatedService")
        public static class BindIsolatedService extends MethodHook {
            protected Object hook(...) { args[6] = null;
                                         return BindServiceCommon(who,method,args,7); }
        }

WHY THE STRIPPING HOOK NEVER FIRES ON API 35
    ClassInvocationStub#initAnnotation registers a hook by *name* against the methods of
    the interface being proxied. IActivityManager has no bindIsolatedService on API 35, so
    @ProxyMethod("bindIsolatedService") binds to nothing and BindIsolatedService is dead
    code. Every isolated bind arrives at the bindServiceInstance hook instead, which
    forwards args[6] untouched.

ARGUMENT INDEX
    Confirmed against the device's framework.jar rather than assumed. The dex descriptor
    for IActivityManager.bindServiceInstance on this build is:

        (Landroid/app/IApplicationThread;   0 caller
         Landroid/os/IBinder;               1 token
         Landroid/content/Intent;           2 service
         Ljava/lang/String;                 3 resolvedType
         Landroid/app/IServiceConnection;   4 connection
         J                                  5 flags
         Ljava/lang/String;                 6 instanceName
         Ljava/lang/String;                 7 callingPackage
         I)I                                8 userId

    args[6] is the instance name and args[7] the calling package, which is the index
    BindServiceCommon already rewrites. Both sibling hooks pass callingPackageIndex 7, so
    both were written for this same layout.

FIX
    Clear args[6] in the hook that actually runs, exactly as the dead sibling already does.
    The bind then presents no instance name, and ActivityManagerService accepts it.

SCOPE AND LIMITS
    Ordinary Context.bindService() on API 31+ also reaches AMS through bindServiceInstance,
    always with a null instance name, so clearing an already-null field is a no-op on that
    path. Only isolated binds change behaviour.

    This does NOT provide real isolated processes, and does not reproduce the multiple
    concurrent renderers a mature container achieves. ActiveServices#bindService picks a
    proxy slot from serviceInfo.processName alone, so every instance of
    SandboxedProcessService0 collapses into the single :p1 slot. Instance-aware slot
    allocation is a separate, larger change and is deliberately not attempted here.

    No uid, package name, signature, permission or caller identity is altered. The change
    only removes a parameter from a request the guest makes of the host, which narrows
    rather than widens what the guest can ask for. AMS continues to apply its own checks
    to the proxy component.

diff --git a/Bcore/src/main/java/top/niunaijun/blackbox/fake/service/IActivityManagerProxy.java b/Bcore/src/main/java/top/niunaijun/blackbox/fake/service/IActivityManagerProxy.java
index 4f571d1..5b17d68 100644
--- a/Bcore/src/main/java/top/niunaijun/blackbox/fake/service/IActivityManagerProxy.java
+++ b/Bcore/src/main/java/top/niunaijun/blackbox/fake/service/IActivityManagerProxy.java
@@ -426,6 +426,12 @@ public class IActivityManagerProxy extends ClassInvocationStub {
     public static class bindServiceInstance extends MethodHook {
         @Override
         protected Object hook(Object who, Method method, Object[] args) throws Throwable {
+            // args[6] is instanceName. Context.bindIsolatedService() reaches
+            // IActivityManager through this method -- IActivityManager itself declares no
+            // bindIsolatedService, so the sibling hook below never runs -- and the guest's
+            // instance name would then be applied to a ProxyService$PN, which is not an
+            // isolated service. ActivityManagerService rejects that combination.
+            args[6] = null;
             return BindServiceCommon(who,method,args,7);
         }
 
